</p><h2><a name=3D"User_Notes"></a> User Notes </h2>
 Because the code is matlab, one can easily change it for whatever=20
purpose one may like, and it is meant to easily integrate into a=20
standard matlab working environment.
<p>
There is an example of how to run the code in a script called peramx.m.=20
Using the mex files "solvetri" and "matrc" for your particular machine=20
will make ram just as fast (or perhaps a little faster) than the=20
original fortran version.
</p><p>
J.C.: One good test I have found in a pe code is whether it propagates a
 mode without introducing numerical coupling; I'll try that on your RAM=20
for various grids. M.D.: I have done the test of propagating a high=20
angle mode with ram and it seems to work. As you say, this is a good way
 to check if the grid size is adequate.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Comments_on_Accuracy"></a> Comments on Accuracy </h2>
<p>
Subject: Re: RAM Matlab Code
</p><p>
Brian, It was not my intent to alarm everyone, but the details of range=20
dependent modeling in acoustics is still a research issue. Even Mike=20
Collins would agree. My only suggestion is to make comparisons among the
 various versions of RAM, i.e. different Pade coefficients, and make=20
comparisons to CSNAP and other range coupled normal mode codes.
</p><p>
