<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Parameters"></a> Parameters </h2>
 Accurate calculations depend on a number of parameters, and as always=20
there is a trade off between speed and accuracy. It is also true that=20
phase errors accumulate, so if a grid is good at 100 km, it may not be=20
good at 1000 km.
<p> </p><ul>
<li> <strong>np</strong>: The number of pade coefficients. The=20
calculation is not very sensitive at all to to this number, and np=3D4=20
always seems to be a good choice.
</li> <li> <strong>deltaz</strong>: The depth increment for the PE grid.
 Picking the correct grid size for ultimate speed is important. While a=20
coarser grid is faster, it will not be as accurate. A deltaz=3D1.0 is=20
O.K., while a deltaz=3D0.2 will produce more accurate results but take an=
=20
order of magnitude longer to complete. This value is also dependent on=20
frequency, with higher frequencies demanding smaller values. For fc=3D250=
=20
Hz, deltaz=3D0.2 is probably closer to what is needed. A calculation at=20
ca. 1500 km range and 75 Hz required a deltaz of 0.5 for an accurate=20
result; deltaz=3D1.0 resulted in 10-20 ms increased dispersal of the=20
arrival pattern.
</li> <li> <strong>deltar</strong>: The range increment for the PE grid.
 The code is less sensitive to the range step, but this value should be=20
set small enough so that the PE converges. Also, N*deltar should equal=20
the sound-speed horizontal sampling distance, where N is an integer. So=20
if you've used a sound speed grid of 1000m, deltar=3D250 should work, but=
=20
if you use a sound speed grid of 400m, then set deltar=3D200m. The value=20
of deltar is inversely proportional to computation time.
</li> <li> <strong>T</strong>: The time window. This value sets the=20
width of the time window for the calcuations. The value of this window=20
sets the number of frequencies that are required to do the calculation,=20
hence it is directly related to the computation costs. The value should=20
be tuned to the expected dispersal of the arrival pattern. Too large a=20
value just means that more frequencies than needed have to be=20
calculated.
</li> <li> <strong>dzm</strong>: The depth interval used for output=20
depth decimation. This number is not related to the calculation, per se,
 but is related to how coarsely the results are subsampled for plotting.
 Too small a value results in a grainy looking figure.
</li></ul>=20
<p> </p><ul>
<li> <strong>fc</strong>: The center frequency desired. 75 Hz for ATOC-like=
 transmissions, 250-Hz for HLF-5-like transmissions.
</li> <li> <strong>fs</strong>: Sample frequency, generally 4X the center f=
requency.
</li> <li> <strong>N</strong>: The number of frequencies for the calculatio=
n=3Dfs*T. (not a user specified parameter, except through T.)
</li> <li> <strong>df</strong>: The frequency increment=3Dfs/N. (not a user=
 specified parameter, except through T.)
</li></ul>=20
<p> </p><ul>
<li> <strong>tdelay</strong>: The time delay, which sets the start time=20
for the window of width T. This value should be roughly the range=20
divided by a nominal sound speed, c0. c0=3D1485 or 1490 m/s. The value of=
=20
tdelay is used to place the prediction in the correct (unaliased) time=20
window.
</li></ul>=20
<p> </p><ul>
<li> <strong>attn</strong>: Attenuation. The default attenuation is 0.5=20
db/wavelength 100m below the bottom, and 5db/wavelength 300m below the=20
bottom. Bottom reflections are/were discouraged in this code.=20
Attenuation is a two row matrix, the top row being attenuation 100 m=20
below the bottom (0.5), the second row row being below that (5). The=20
exact parameterizations vary wildly in the actual ocean, and are poorly=20
modeled, in any case. The default choices for sediment density and speed
 are unrealistic, but since the attenuation is so high one wouldn't=20
notice. A somewhat more realistic WAG (Wild Ass Guess) as opposed to the
 SWAG (Stupid Wild Ass Guess) that I used might be rho=3D1.1 kg/l, cs=3D170=
0
 m/s.
</li></ul>=20
<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Mex_file_compilation"></a> Mex file compilation </h2>
 The package includes two C routines, matrc.c and solvetri.c, that=20
should be compiled to obtain mex-files for these functions. These=20
routines will allow the RAM code to run more than an order of magnitude=20
faster. There are two ways to compile these routines:
<p> </p><ul>
<li> Place the file "mexopts.sh" in the local directory and edit it for=20
your own situation. The file has sections for x86, xa64, and solaris=20
architectures, and matlab will automatically select the correct=20
architecture for the computer you are on. Set CC, CXX, or FC to be your=20
C-compiler, C++-compiler, or FORTRAN compiler, respectively. You can, in
 addition, set whatever optimization flags there (e.g., "COPTIMFLAGS")=20
you'd like to try. With that file in the local directory, "mex matrc.c"=20
will compile the mex file, using the options in this local file=20
(otherwise it uses the default version in matlab's installation=20
directory). Use the "-v" option, if you want to see what is going on.
</li></ul>=20
<p> </p><ul>
<li> Matt D. has put together a makefile that is also suitable to=20
compile the mex files. Edit this file to set your compiler and=20
optimization flags, and then execute "make all", which should compile=20
the mex files and link in the appropriate libraries. This makefile is=20
set up for the x86 architecture, and it will require significant=20
modification to work on the xa64 architecture.
</li></ul>=20
<p>
Portland group compiler options include "-fastsse -tp p7", while gnu=20
compiler options include "-O3 -mfpmath=3Dsse -msse -march=3Dathlon-xp", or=
=20
"-O3 -mfpmath=3Dsse -msse2 -march=3Dk8" for AMD64 cpus. In linux, "cat=20
/proc/cpuinfo" will tell you what SSE support your cpu has. "Your=20
mileage may vary."
</p>
<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Range_Dependent_Sound_Speed"></a> Range Dependent Sound =
Speed </h2>
 Range dependent sound speed is implemented by defining variables:  <ul>
<li> rp - ranges of profiles in meters
</li> <li> nrp - number of profiles
</li> <li> cw - matrix of sound speeds Each row is a sound speed profile.
</li> <li> zw - depth of sound speeds in meters (positive down)
</li></ul>=20
In general, I think it is true that the zw have to be at a fine=20
increment of O(1m), and the same zw should be used for all profiles.=20
Adding range dependence will make the code run considerably slower. The=20
following segment of matlab code will produce values appropriate for ram
 from the sound speeds derived from the matlab mapping package. The=20
mapping package extracts sound speed from the WOA on 33 standard depths.
 <blockquote>=20
<pre>load temp.ssp I=3D(temp(:,1)=3D=3D-1); rp=3Dtemp(I,2)*1000; nrp=3Dleng=
th(rp);=20
zq=3Dtemp(2:34,1); cw=3Dtemp(:,2); cw=3Dreshape(cw,34,nrp); cw(1,:)=3D[]; z=
w=3D0:1:5000;=20
zw=3Dzw(:); cw=3Dinterp1(zq,cw,zw,'spline'); </pre> </blockquote>
 cw is a matrix, while rp and zw are a vectors. While it is not strictly
 necessary to interpolate sound speed to the zw, you should interpolate=20
on your own. RAM will just linearly interpolate sound speed to the grid=20
size specified to deltaz. A coarsely sampled sound speed could therefore
 lead to biases, false caustics, war with Iran, etc.
<p>
The source depth, sound speeds and depths require the flat-earth transforma=
tion before sending to RAM (see also downloads).
</p><blockquote>=20
<pre>% Flat earth transform: Re=3D6378137; invRe=3D1/Re; eps=3Dzw*invRe;=20
zw=3Dzw.*(1+(1/2)*eps+(1/3)*eps.*eps); eps=3Deps*ones(1,nrp); cw=3Dcw.*(1+e=
ps+eps.*eps);=20
cw=3Dcw';    % ram expects sound speed profiles in rows.  eps=3Dzsrc*invRe;=
=20
zsrc=3Dzsrc*(1+(1/2)*eps+(1/3)*eps*eps);  </pre> </blockquote>
<p>
To include the effects of internal waves in this calculation, sound=20
speed has to be defined on fine enough depth and range increments to=20
resolve the internal waves of interest. This makes cw a rather large=20
matrix.
</p>
<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Range_Dependent_Bathymetry"></a> Range Dependent Bathyme=
try </h2>
 Implementing range-dependent bathymetry is easy. The code just needs=20
vectors "rb" and "zb", which are the bathymetry ranges and bathymetry=20
values (both in meters), respectively, along the path of interest. The=20
bathymetry extracted using the mapping package can be loaded in by the=20
following simple matlab code: <blockquote> <pre>load temp.bth rb=3Dtemp(:,1=
); zb=3Dtemp(:,2); </pre> </blockquote>
<p>
Adding bathymetry, other than flat bottom, to the calculation will slow=20
it significantly - by a factor of 5 or more. matrc needs to run every=20
time there is an environmental change (sound speed or bathymetry), so=20
the rough bottom case will take longer than the flat bottom case.
</p><p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"Parallelization_Notes"></a> Parallelization Notes </h2>
 The computations involve completely separate calculations at the=20
different frequencies of the broadband signal, hence the parallelization
 problem in this case is trivial. One need merely farm out various sets=20
of frequencies to whatever computers one has available, let them run=20
their calculations, and then collect the results at the end. The code=20
has an implementation of parallelization using matlab's tcpip toolbox=20
already implemented, but there are other ways to do this.
<p>
The parallelization just has to fill the columns of the matrix <em>psif</em=
>.
 So, one way to parallelize the calculation would be to have each=20
processor fill different columns, and then collect the results after=20
they are all done.
</p>
<p>
</p><h2><a name=3D"FAQ"></a> FAQ </h2> <ul>
<li> Can this RAM do 3-D? <em>RAM cannot do azimuthal coupling although the=
 scaling is set up for a 3-d non-coupled problem.</em>
</li> <li> The final figure is rather grainy. How would I boost the resolut=
ion of the arrival depth-travel time figure? <em>You probably have the deci=
mation factor, dzm, set higher than you'd like. The output grid size is dzm=
*deltaz.</em>
</li> <li> How come the travel times predicted by RAM are way off=20
compared to a similar ray prediction, yet the arrival patterns are the=20
same? <em>The RAM code makes a prediction for a time window "T", and=20
that time window is aliased infinitely many times. Therefore, you need=20
to apply a reasonably accurate time delay to put the time window "T" in=20
the physically correct time. This involves giving the code a reasonably=20
accurate value for "tdelay", which roughly the range divided by a=20
nominal sound speed. For the Pacific, a nominal sound speed is ca. 1485=20
m/s, while a larger value is more appropriate for the Atlantic. The=20
travel times predicted by RAM are correct, if you set the time window=20
such that the physically correct arrival pattern (rather than an alias)=20
appears in it. It is helpful to compare the RAM result to a ray=20
prediction to be sure a good value for the time delay is being used.</em>
</li> <li> How come the travel times predicted by RAM are way off compared =
to a similar ray prediction? <em>The
 RAM code uses the unmodified sound speed that is input to calculate the
 propagation, whereas most ray codes (e.g., eigenray) apply a flat-earth
 transformation before calculating the propagation. The flat-earth=20
transformation accounts for the spherical earth; sound speeds and depth=20
are stretched slightly to be equivalent to propagation on a sphere. So=20
try applying the flat-earth transformation to your sound speeds before=20
doing the RAM calculation (see eflat.m download above), or turning off=20
the flat-earth transformation of your ray code, if you can.</em>
</li></ul>=20
